WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.040
Slide the handle on the front of your camera.

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.495
The dot wheel turns, and the hole behind the lens changes size.

3
00:00:08.245 --> 00:00:10.705
Three holes, three sizes.

4
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:14.768
Now, photographers have names for holes like these,

5
00:00:14.518 --> 00:00:18.648
and the names are numbers: eight, eleven, sixteen.

6
00:00:18.398 --> 00:00:20.943
Two things about them are strange.

7
00:00:20.693 --> 00:00:23.688
The biggest number means the smallest hole.

8
00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:26.743
And the steps between them look completely random.

9
00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:30.197
So why do camera numbers go eight, eleven, sixteen?

10
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.840
First, decide: which lets in more light, f/8 or f/16, and by how much?

11
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:40.845
Most people guess that f/16 lets in twice the light of f/8,

12
00:00:40.595 --> 00:00:42.840
because sixteen is twice eight.

13
00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.045
It's actually the other way round.

14
00:00:44.795 --> 00:00:49.090
An f/16 hole is half as wide as an f/8 hole.

15
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.785
And half as wide doesn't let in half the light.

16
00:00:51.535 --> 00:00:53.042
It lets in a quarter.

17
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:58.822
That's because light comes through the whole area of a hole, not just across its width.

18
00:00:58.572 --> 00:01:00.632
Picture a square hole.

19
00:01:00.382 --> 00:01:06.028
Make it twice as wide, and it's twice as tall too, so it has four times the area.

20
00:01:05.778 --> 00:01:11.028
Round holes play by the same rule: twice the width, four times the light.

21
00:01:10.778 --> 00:01:13.092
So if you only want to double the light,

22
00:01:12.842 --> 00:01:15.958
you need a step that's smaller than doubling the width.

23
00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:19.083
What number, multiplied by itself, makes two?

24
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:21.208
It's about 1.41.

25
00:01:20.958 --> 00:01:25.623
Make a hole 1.41 times wider, and you get twice the light.

26
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:27.793
Now the numbers.

27
00:01:27.543 --> 00:01:35.538
Take the length of the lens, divide it by the width of the hole, and that's the f-number.

28
00:01:35.288 --> 00:01:37.253
Your lens is 32 millimetres.

29
00:01:37.003 --> 00:01:41.056
A hole 2 millimetres wide gives you 32 divided by 2: f/16.

30
00:01:40.806 --> 00:01:46.063
In 1867, two men called Sutton and Dawson wrote a hole this way,

31
00:01:45.813 --> 00:01:48.316
as a fraction of the lens's length.

32
00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:53.867
It means one number stands for one brightness, on any lens, long or short.

33
00:01:53.617 --> 00:02:02.587
Now keep dividing by 1.41, again and again: 16, 11.3, 8, 5.7, 4.

34
00:02:02.337 --> 00:02:05.337
Round those off and you have the famous row.

35
00:02:05.087 --> 00:02:08.192
Every step along the row doubles the light.

36
00:02:07.942 --> 00:02:10.361
Every second step doubles the width.

37
00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:17.022
A picture needs a certain amount of light, and that amount is how bright, times how long.

38
00:02:16.772 --> 00:02:21.866
Twice the light through the hole for half the time gives you exactly the same picture.

39
00:02:21.616 --> 00:02:26.361
Your camera has only one time, about a three-hundredth of a second.

40
00:02:26.111 --> 00:02:28.736
So the dots have to do the choosing.

41
00:02:28.486 --> 00:02:34.240
In 1890, Hurter and Driffield measured how much light makes a plate go how dark.

42
00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:42.215
Film speed numbers came out of that: the ASA scale in 1943, and ISO from 1974.

43
00:02:41.965 --> 00:02:45.404
Double the number, and the film needs half the light.

44
00:02:45.154 --> 00:02:47.958
Your film says ISO 400.

45
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:51.977
There's an old rule photographers carry in their heads.

46
00:02:51.727 --> 00:02:57.587
In full sun, use f/16, and for the time, one over the film speed.

47
00:02:57.337 --> 00:03:02.834
For ISO 400 that's f/16 for a four-hundredth of a second.

48
00:03:02.584 --> 00:03:07.252
Your small dot is about f/16, and your time is a three-hundredth,

49
00:03:07.002 --> 00:03:09.679
which by the model is half a step over.

50
00:03:09.429 --> 00:03:12.949
And remember, film forgives three steps too much.

51
00:03:12.699 --> 00:03:16.664
So the chart on your camera says: full sun, small dot.

52
00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:20.247
Now let's check it with your own three dots.

53
00:03:19.997 --> 00:03:26.237
They're 1.96, 2.88 and 3.34 millimetres wide.

54
00:03:25.987 --> 00:03:33.342
From small to middle: 2.88 divided by 1.96 is 1.47.

55
00:03:33.092 --> 00:03:37.432
Multiply that by itself and you get 2.16.

56
00:03:37.182 --> 00:03:39.062
About double the light.

57
00:03:38.812 --> 00:03:48.841
From middle to large: 3.34 divided by 2.88 is 1.16, and times itself that's 1.35.

58
00:03:48.591 --> 00:03:50.331
About a third more.

59
00:03:50.081 --> 00:03:58.422
And as f-numbers, 32 divided by each width comes out at about f/16, f/11 and f/9.5.

60
00:03:59.133 --> 00:04:00.968
So here's your whole chart.

61
00:04:00.718 --> 00:04:02.883
Full sun, small dot.

62
00:04:02.633 --> 00:04:04.613
Hazy sun, middle dot.

63
00:04:04.363 --> 00:04:06.658
Bright cloud, large dot.

64
00:04:06.408 --> 00:04:12.054
And if it's darker than that, you take a time picture: by a window, small dot, count one.

65
00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:17.757
Why does the large dot stop at f/9.5 and not go on to f/8?

66
00:04:17.507 --> 00:04:20.847
f/8 would need a hole 4 millimetres wide.

67
00:04:20.597 --> 00:04:26.069
But a single lens goes soft away from the middle, and a bigger hole makes that worse.

68
00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:26.881
By the model,

69
00:04:26.631 --> 00:04:31.879
a far scene is postcard-sharp across nearly all of the picture at the small dot.

70
00:04:31.629 --> 00:04:36.129
At the large dot, it's sharp across about six parts in ten.

71
00:04:35.879 --> 00:04:38.669
A bigger hole would cost even more.

72
00:04:38.419 --> 00:04:42.479
Every setting is a trade: more light, less sharpness.

73
00:04:42.229 --> 00:04:45.075
It's the pinhole's bargain all over again.

74
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.300
So why 8, 11, 16?

75
00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:53.770
Each step doubles the light, and doubling the light takes 1.41 times the width.

76
00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.520
You can watch this happen on your own face.

77
00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:02.028
Look in a mirror as you switch the light off and on: your pupil is your eye's own dot.

78
00:05:01.778 --> 00:05:05.810
And try this: take the back off, with no film inside,

79
00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:09.448
and watch a window on tracing paper as you change dots.

80
00:05:09.198 --> 00:05:11.268
That's the hole sorted out.

81
00:05:11.018 --> 00:05:13.408
But the hole is only half of it.

82
00:05:13.158 --> 00:05:17.770
The other half is time, and your camera does that with a piece of plastic.

83
00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.970
So next time: how do you make plastic move that fast, the same way every time?

84
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.215
Every picture here is drawn or simulated.

85
00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:29.173
No photograph has been taken with this camera yet.

